{0 How to use ctypes in a dune project}

@author Etienne Millon

{1 About this guide}

This guide assumes that you have a working dune project and will show you how
to add bindings to a C library. This guide does not use the experimental ctypes
support for [dune], but uses manual rules.

In this example, we suppose our project is an image manipulation library called
[images] and we will be adding support to the PNG image format using the
[libpng] library.

{1 Create a library}

Create a library called [libpng] that will abstract away the build system
aspects of using stub generation.

Create the following file hierarchy:

{@text[
lib/libpng/bindings/dune
lib/libpng/bindings/png_bindings.ml
lib/libpng/generator/generator.ml
lib/libpng/generator/dune
lib/libpng/generated/dune
lib/libpng/type_generator/dune
lib/libpng/dune
lib/libpng/libpng.ml
]}

More information about this [dune] setup can be found in
{{!page-how_stub_generation_works}this explanation.

{2 File contents}

{3 [lib/libpng/bindings/dune]}

{@dune[
(library
 (name png_bindings)
 (package images)
 (libraries ctypes))
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/bindings/png_bindings.ml]}

Add bindings to the relevant functions here using the [F] and [T] modules.

{[
module Generate (F : Ctypes.FOREIGN) = struct
  (* ... *)
end

module Generate_types (T : Ctypes.TYPE) = struct
  (* ... *)
end
]}

{%html:<details>
<summary>Concrete example with libpng</summary>
%}

{[
module Generate (F : Ctypes.FOREIGN) = struct
  type image

  let image_ : image Ctypes.structure Ctypes.typ = Ctypes.structure "png_image"
  let image = Ctypes.typedef image_ "png_image"
  let opaque = Ctypes.field image "opaque" (Ctypes.ptr Ctypes.void)
  let version = Ctypes.field image "version" Ctypes.uint32_t
  let width = Ctypes.field image "width" Ctypes.uint32_t
  let height = Ctypes.field image "height" Ctypes.uint32_t
  let () = Ctypes.seal image_

  let png_image_begin_read_from_file =
    F.foreign "png_image_begin_read_from_file"
      F.(Ctypes.ptr image @-> Ctypes.ptr Ctypes.char @-> returning Ctypes.int)
end

module Generate_types (T : Ctypes.TYPE) = struct
  let png_image_version = T.constant "PNG_IMAGE_VERSION" T.uint32_t
end
]}

{%html:</details> %}


{3 [lib/libpng/generator/dune]}

{@dune[
(executable
 (name generator)
 (libraries ctypes.stubs png_bindings))
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/generator/generator.ml]}

If the C library needs particular [#include] directives, they need to be specified here.
 
{[
let () =
  match Sys.argv with
  | [| _; "ml" |] ->
      Cstubs.write_ml Format.std_formatter ~prefix:"images_png"
        (module Png_bindings.Generate)
  | [| _; "c" |] ->
      Printf.printf "#include <png.h>\n";
      Cstubs.write_c Format.std_formatter ~prefix:"images_png"
        (module Png_bindings.Generate)
  | [| _; "types" |] ->
      Cstubs.Types.write_c Format.std_formatter
        (module Png_bindings.Generate_types)
  | _ -> assert false
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/generated/dune]}

If the C library needs particular [-L] or [-l] flags, they need to be specified
here.

{@dune[
(library
 (name png_generated)
 (package images)
 (libraries ctypes.stubs)
 (c_library_flags :standard -lpng)
 (foreign_stubs
  (language c)
  (names png_generated_stubs)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  png_generated.ml
  (run ../generator/generator.exe ml)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  png_generated_stubs.c
  (run ../generator/generator.exe c)))
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/type_generator/dune]}

The [#include] directives to access the library will need to be specified here
too. One rule needs to access the location of [ctypes] C headers. This approach
might break if [ctypes] is vendored in your dune workspace but as of Dune 3.18
there is no good solution in that case.

{@dune[
(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  type_generator.c
  (progn
   (echo "#include <png.h>\n")
   (run ../generator/generator.exe types))))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  ctypes-location.txt
  (run dirname %{lib:ctypes:ctypes_cstubs_internals.h})))

(rule
 (target types_generator.exe)
 (action
  (run
   %{cc}
   -I
   %{read-strings:ctypes-location.txt}
   -o
   %{target}
   %{dep:type_generator.c})))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  png_types_generated.ml
  (run ./types_generator.exe)))

(library
 (name png_types_generated)
 (package images)
 (libraries ctypes))
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/dune]}

{@dune[
(library
 (name libpng)
 (libraries png_bindings png_generated png_types_generated)
 (package images))
]}

{3 [lib/libpng/libpng.ml]}

This module will reexport both kinds of bindings.

{[
include Png_bindings.Generate (Png_generated)
include Png_bindings.Generate_types (Png_types_generated)
]}

{2 Aside: using a private library as a dependency of a public library}

We've used libraries to make our build more modular. This can be a problem if
the larger project ([images] in our example) is a public library, because the
inner libraries are not public API. By adding [(package images)] to them and
not setting a [(public_name)], we install them but without making their
interface public.

If your main project is an executable or a non-public library, the `(package)`
fields can be left out.

{2 Aside: if you're not using type bindings}

If you're not using type bindings ({e ie}, the body of the [Generate_types] functor is left empty), you can remove:

- the [Generate_types] functor;
- the ["types"] branch in [generator.ml];
- the [type_generator] directory;
- mentions of [png_types_generated] in [lib/libpng/dune] and [lib/libpng/libpng.ml]

{1 Use this library}

Now, use this library in your existing code. Add [libpng] to [(libraries ...)]
in [lib/dune] and create [lib/png.ml].

In this new module, define high-level functions that use the [ctypes] API and
the values from [Libpng].

{%html:<details>

<summary>Concrete example with libpng</summary>
%}

This function reads an image using [libpng] and returns its dimensions.

{[
let of_file ~path =
  let image = Ctypes.make Libpng.image in
  Ctypes.setf image Libpng.opaque Ctypes.null;
  Ctypes.setf image Libpng.version Libpng.png_image_version;
  let cpath = Ctypes.CArray.of_string path in
  let r =
    Libpng.png_image_begin_read_from_file (Ctypes.addr image)
      (Ctypes.CArray.start cpath)
  in
  if r = 0 then None
  else
    let width = Unsigned.UInt32.to_int32 (Ctypes.getf image Libpng.width) in
    let height = Unsigned.UInt32.to_int32 (Ctypes.getf image Libpng.height) in
    Some { Metadata.width; height }
]}

{%html:</details> %}

{1 Update the project metadata}

Your project now uses [ctypes], so add it as a dependency in your [opam] files
(directly or by modifying [dune-project]).

{1 Conclusion}

We've seen how to add new [ctypes] bindings to an existing project. By using
plain dune rules, you have a better guarantee that this won't break in the
future.
